    <div class="prose">
      <p>Earlier this year, I signed up for what looked like the solution to all my DJ problems: a premium music subscription promising seamless software integration. Three clicks and 120 dollars later, I had a year long subscription and was feeling pretty good about my life choices.</p>
      <p>Until nothing worked.</p>
      <p>After twenty minutes of rage Googling, I found it. Buried in section sixteen of the terms: integration limited to one specific brand of equipment. Mine was not that brand. Not even close.</p>
      <p>The customer service rep asked if I had tried turning it off and on again, then delivered the real answer: it was clearly stated in the terms I agreed to. No refund. My 120 dollars, gone. My faith in the idea that anyone wrote those terms for me to actually read, also gone.</p>
      <p>I watched a friend do the same thing with her own contract months later: hands up after the fifth "notwithstanding the foregoing," the leap from "I don't understand any of this" to "so my payment is due on the 15th" landing like a small act of relief, not a formality.</p>
      <p class="pull">That failure produced a question, not just a complaint: if a legally valid clause can remain practically invisible to the person agreeing to it, where does the failure actually occur? The reader, the lawyer, or the document itself?</p>
      <p>This project is an attempt to answer that question with something more durable than a strong opinion. What follows is the experiment, the framework it produced, and the open questions it has not resolved yet.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

