      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="lede" style="color:#b3ab97;max-width:62ch;">Most work at the intersection of AI and law asks whether a model can draft accurately. Accuracy is close to solved. The unexamined question is whether accuracy and comprehensibility are even the same axis, or whether a contract can be perfectly enforceable and perfectly opaque at the same time.</p>
    <p style="font-size:22px;font-weight:800;max-width:24ch;line-height:1.4;margin-top:8px;">AI can draft a perfect contract. It will never know what it feels like to be on the other side of one.</p>
    <p style="color:#b3ab97;font-size:15px;max-width:62ch;margin-top:22px;">This project treats that gap, between legal validity and human comprehension, as the actual research object. The Human Law Index below is a first attempt to measure it directly rather than assume a well-drafted clause and a well-understood clause are the same thing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div id="colors">
<div class="color-exhibit blue reveal">
  <div class="wrap-wide">
    <div class="color-head"><div><div class="tag">Exhibit C1 &middot; Psychology of Color</div><h2>Blue. The quiet overachiever.</h2></div><div class="tag">Alert, emphasis, precision, inverted.</div></div>
    <div class="color-body">
      <p>Blue does not shout, but it gets results. It helps people read faster, remember more, and feel at ease, and in a world of shrinking attention spans, that calm clarity is everything.</p>
      <p class="quote">&ldquo;Trustworthy&rdquo; was not just the top response for blue. It was the dominant one.</p>
      <p>The association runs deep: the sky, the ocean, the things that represent stability in the natural world. A blue header does not just organize content, it establishes hierarchy before a single word is read.</p>
    </div>
    <span class="pilot-tag">Pilot observation, not peer reviewed</span>
    <div class="color-stats">
      <div class="color-stat"><span class="num" data-count="88">0</span>%<span class="cap">Participants who recalled a blue-framed clause after 48 hours, small sample</span></div>
      <div class="color-stat"><span class="num" data-count="70">0</span>%<span class="cap">Associated blue with reliability, informal survey</span></div>
      <div class="color-stat"><span class="num" data-count="550">0</span> m/s<span class="cap">Median recognition time, internal test set</span></div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:14px;opacity:.85;max-width:60ch;">Most documents fail because they treat color as decoration, not communication architecture. Use blue for structure. Save red for focus.</p>
    <div class="cite" style="border-color:rgba(255,255,255,.3);color:#dbe6fb;">Directional support: colour-memory associations documented in Dzulkifli &amp; Mustafar (2013) and Hall &amp; Hanna (2004); figures above are this project's own small-sample testing, not drawn from those studies.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="color-exhibit red reveal">
  <div class="wrap-wide">
    <div class="color-head"><div><div class="tag">Exhibit C2 &middot; Psychology of Color</div><h2>Red. Alert, emphasis, precision.</h2></div><div class="tag">If everything is red, nothing is.</div></div>
    <span class="pilot-tag">Pilot observation, not peer reviewed</span>
    <div class="color-stats">
      <div class="color-stat"><span class="num" data-count="90">0</span>%<span class="cap">Highest recall rate of the three colors in this project's own testing</span></div>
      <div class="color-stat"><span class="num" data-count="470">0</span> m/s<span class="cap">Fastest median recognition time, internal test set</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="color-body">
      <p>Red grabs attention, fast. It is broadly associated in the cognitive psychology literature with threat and urgency detection, which is exactly why it is the most misused color in legal design.</p>
      <p>Most people think red creates urgency, so they use it for everything. In practice it tends to create an urgent feeling to close the document and walk away. The rule this project follows: if everything is urgent, nothing is.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="use-cases">
      <div class="use-case"><span class="n">Use for</span><p>Specific deadlines, delivery methods, and numbers or dates that trigger legal obligations.</p></div>
      <div class="use-case"><span class="n">Use for</span><p>Risk indicators. Red borders around clauses that shift significant liability, reserved for the highest risk provisions only.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="avoid-list">
      <div>Do not use red for headers, titles, or full paragraphs. It reduces readability.</div>
      <div>Do not use red in visuals unless denoting loss, risk, or non-compliance.</div>
      <div>Do not use red for reviewer comments. It desensitizes readers to its real alert function.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="color-exhibit yellow reveal">
  <div class="wrap-wide">
    <div class="color-head"><div><div class="tag">Exhibit C3 &middot; Psychology of Color</div><h2>Yellow. Notice, process, remember.</h2></div><div class="tag">Like red, but with better people skills.</div></div>
    <span class="pilot-tag">Pilot observation, not peer reviewed</span>
    <div class="color-stats">
      <div class="color-stat"><span class="num" data-count="88">0</span>%<span class="cap">Recall rate for yellow-highlighted clauses, small sample</span></div>
      <div class="color-stat"><span class="num" data-count="475">0</span> m/s<span class="cap">Median recognition time, internal test set</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="color-body">
      <p>Warm colors, yellow in particular, are associated in the literature with attention capture without the stress response red tends to trigger, which makes it well suited to cognitive engagement rather than alarm. It does not yell, and that distinction matters in legal design.</p>
      <p class="quote">Yellow does not warn. It guides.</p>
      <p>A clause buried in paragraph forty seven goes unread. Highlight the same line in yellow, and people suddenly see what they are entitled to, not just what they are stuck with.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="use-cases">
      <div class="use-case"><span class="n">Highlight user choice</span><p>Optional actions, rights, and non-mandatory elements. Where readers actually have power.</p></div>
      <div class="use-case"><span class="n">Reveal hidden consequences</span><p>Auto-renewals, interest charges, the quiet risks people skim past.</p></div>
      <div class="use-case"><span class="n">Emphasize benefits</span><p>Shift the document from what you cannot do to what you get.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="avoid-list">
      <div>Do not highlight entire paragraphs. It strains the eyes and kills readability.</div>
      <div>Do not pair yellow with white or light backgrounds. It disappears.</div>
      <div>Do not use yellow for deadlines or terminations. That is red's job.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</div>

